  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-6.1-1", question: "Which is the syllabus formula for the trade payables turnover (days)?", choices: ["Trade payables ÷ credit purchases × 365", "Trade payables ÷ credit sales × 365", "Credit purchases ÷ trade payables × 365", "Trade payables ÷ cost of sales × 365"], explanation: "Trade payables arise from credit purchases, so they are divided by credit purchases and multiplied by 365. Credit sales belong to the trade receivables ratio, the inverted fraction gives a meaningless figure, and cost of sales includes the change in inventory, so it is not the syllabus denominator." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-2", question: "Revenue was $90,000 and cost of sales $63,000. What is the gross profit margin?", choices: ["30.00%", "42.86%", "70.00%", "27.00%"], explanation: "Gross profit is 27,000; 27,000 ÷ 90,000 × 100 = 30.00%. 42.86% is the mark-up (27,000 ÷ 63,000), 70% is cost of sales as a percentage of revenue, and 27% confuses the dollar amount with a percentage." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-3", question: "Revenue was $75,000 and cost of sales $60,000. What is the mark-up?", choices: ["25.00%", "20.00%", "80.00%", "125.00%"], explanation: "Gross profit is 15,000 and mark-up = gross profit ÷ cost of sales × 100 = 15,000 ÷ 60,000 × 100 = 25.00%. 20.00% is the gross profit margin (15,000 ÷ 75,000), 80.00% is cost of sales as a percentage of revenue, and 125.00% is revenue divided by cost of sales." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-4", question: "Current assets are $36,000, including inventory of $15,000. Current liabilities are $24,000. What is the current (working capital) ratio?", choices: ["1.50:1", "0.88:1", "0.67:1", "2.40:1"], explanation: "36,000 ÷ 24,000 = 1.50:1. 0.88:1 is the acid test (liquid) ratio, 0.67:1 inverts the fraction, and 2.40:1 compares total current assets with inventory." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-5", question: "Using the same figures, current assets $36,000 including inventory $15,000 and current liabilities $24,000, what is the acid test (liquid) ratio?", choices: ["0.88:1", "1.50:1", "0.63:1", "2.13:1"], explanation: "(36,000 − 15,000) ÷ 24,000 = 21,000 ÷ 24,000 = 0.88:1. 1.50:1 forgets to remove inventory, 0.63:1 divides inventory by current liabilities, and 2.13:1 adds inventory instead of deducting it." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-6", question: "Cost of sales was $120,000. Inventory was $14,000 at the start of the year and $18,000 at the end. What is the rate of inventory turnover?", choices: ["7.50 times", "6.67 times", "8.57 times", "3.75 times"], explanation: "Average inventory is 16,000; 120,000 ÷ 16,000 = 7.50 times. 6.67 uses closing inventory only, 8.57 uses opening inventory only, and 3.75 divides by the total of the two inventories instead of their average." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-7", question: "Trade receivables at the year end were $18,000. Credit sales were $146,000 and cash sales $36,500. What is the trade receivables turnover (days)?", choices: ["45 days", "36 days", "8 days", "180 days"], explanation: "18,000 ÷ 146,000 × 365 = 45 days. 36 days wrongly adds cash sales to the denominator (18,000 ÷ 182,500 × 365), 8 is roughly the number of times rather than days, and 180 days uses cash sales instead of credit sales." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-8", question: "Cost of sales was $146,000. Inventory was $10,000 at the start of the year and $14,000 at the end. What is the inventory turnover (days)?", choices: ["30 days", "35 days", "60 days", "12 days"], explanation: "Average inventory is (10,000 + 14,000) ÷ 2 = 12,000; 12,000 ÷ 146,000 × 365 = 30 days. 35 days uses closing inventory only, 60 days uses the total of the two inventories instead of their average, and 12 is the rate of inventory turnover in times (146,000 ÷ 12,000 = 12.17), not days." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-9", question: "For a sole trader, what is capital employed in the return on capital employed ratio?", choices: ["Capital plus non-current liabilities", "Capital only", "Capital plus current liabilities", "Total assets less non-current assets"], explanation: "Capital employed is the long-term finance: the owner's capital plus non-current liabilities such as long-term loans. Current liabilities are short-term and are excluded, and total assets less non-current assets is simply current assets." },
    { id: "mcq-6.1-10", question: "Which profit figure is used in the return on capital employed ratio?", choices: ["Profit for the year before interest", "Gross profit", "Profit for the year after interest", "Profit for the year less drawings"], explanation: "Capital employed includes loans, so the profit must be measured before paying the lenders' interest. Gross profit ignores expenses, profit after interest mismatches the base, and drawings are never deducted when measuring profit." },
  ],
