          model: "Inventory is left out because it is the slowest current asset to become cash: it must be sold, often on credit, and then the customer must pay. The acid test (liquid) ratio therefore tests whether short-term debts could be met without relying on future sales. Tamer's credit customers take about 50 days to pay, while he takes about 40 days to pay suppliers who allow only 30 days, so he is already paying late because his cash arrives slowly. He should tighten credit control: send statements promptly, chase overdue accounts and offer a small cash discount for payment within 30 days. Cash would arrive sooner and he could pay his suppliers on time. Drawback: discount allowed reduces profit for the year, and pressing customers may send some of them to competitors.",
          points: ["Inventory must be sold and then collected before it becomes cash (1)", "Acid test (liquid) ratio tests ability to pay without relying on future sales (1)", "Advice linked to the data, such as faster collection from customers who take about 50 days, or holding less inventory (1)", "A drawback of the advice, such as discount reduces profit or customers may be lost (1)"],
