      model: "Profitability: gross profit margin is almost unchanged at 44%, so pricing and buying are fine. Profit margin fell from 14% to 8%, so expenses rose much faster than revenue; the second shop brought rent, wages and loan interest before its sales had grown. ROCE fell from 18% to 10% because the loan increased capital employed while profit fell. Action: review the expenses of the new shop and give it time to build sales; drawback: closing or cutting costs too early could waste the investment. Liquidity: the current (working capital) ratio fell to 1.40:1 and the acid test (liquid) ratio to 0.60:1, so the business could not pay its short-term debts without selling inventory. Inventory turnover fell from 8 to 5 times because two shops now hold inventory, and trade receivables turnover (days) almost doubled to 48 because schools pay slowly, while suppliers are still paid in 40 days. Cash is tied up in inventory and school debts. Actions: chase school accounts and offer a cash discount; hold less inventory in each shop by transferring between them; negotiate 60-day terms with suppliers. Drawbacks: discounts reduce profit, lower inventory may lose sales, suppliers may refuse. Limitation: 2027 is the first year of expansion and the year-end figures may not represent a normal year; non-financial factors such as the location of the second shop also matter. Judgement: the business remains profitable but its liquidity is now weak, and improving cash collection is the priority.",
