  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-6.2-1", question: "A business increased its revenue but its gross profit margin fell. Which is the most likely cause?", choices: ["Selling prices were reduced to attract more customers", "Rent increased during the year", "The owner reduced drawings", "Bank interest charges rose"], explanation: "Gross profit margin depends only on selling prices and the cost of goods sold. Lower prices increase sales volume while reducing the margin on each sale. Rent and interest are expenses that affect profit margin, and drawings affect neither." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-2", question: "Gross profit margin stayed at 40% but profit margin fell from 12% to 8%. What does this show?", choices: ["Expenses rose faster than revenue", "Cost of sales rose faster than revenue", "Selling prices were cut", "Closing inventory was overvalued"], explanation: "A steady gross profit margin rules out changes in prices, cost of sales or inventory valuation. The fall must come after gross profit, which means expenses took a bigger share of revenue. A fall in other income, such as rent received, would have the same effect." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-3", question: "Which action would improve the acid test (liquid) ratio?", choices: ["Selling surplus inventory for cash at cost", "Buying more inventory on credit", "Paying trade payables when the acid test (liquid) ratio is below 1:1", "The owner taking extra drawings in cash"], explanation: "Selling inventory for cash turns a non-liquid asset into a liquid one, raising the ratio. Buying on credit raises liabilities without adding liquid assets. Paying payables when the ratio is below 1:1 reduces top and bottom by the same amount and lowers the ratio further. Drawings remove cash." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-4", question: "The rate of inventory turnover fell from 12 times to 8 times. What does this mean?", choices: ["Goods are held for longer before being sold", "Goods are sold more quickly", "Gross profit margin has increased", "Trade payables are being paid faster"], explanation: "Fewer times means the average inventory takes longer to sell: about 46 days instead of 30. It says nothing directly about margin or payables." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-5", question: "Which change would increase the return on capital employed?", choices: ["Higher profit with the same capital employed", "The owner introducing more capital with the same profit", "Taking a long-term loan and holding the cash in the bank", "Lower profit with the same capital employed"], explanation: "ROCE is profit before interest over capital employed, so more profit on the same funds raises it. Extra capital or a loan held as idle cash increases the bottom of the fraction without adding profit, and lower profit obviously reduces it." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-6", question: "Trade receivables turnover rose from 30 days to 55 days. Which is the most likely explanation?", choices: ["Credit control has weakened or longer credit terms were given", "Customers are paying more quickly", "A larger share of sales is now for cash", "Suppliers are allowing longer credit"], explanation: "More days means credit customers take longer to pay, which points to weaker chasing of debts or more generous terms. Faster payment or more cash sales would reduce the days, and supplier terms affect payables, not receivables." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-7", question: "What is the main risk of allowing trade payables turnover to become very long?", choices: ["Suppliers may refuse further credit or stop supplying", "Trade receivables will increase", "The rate of inventory turnover will rise", "Gross profit margin will automatically fall"], explanation: "Paying late damages the relationship with suppliers, who may demand cash on delivery or withdraw supply. It does not change receivables or inventory turnover, and the margin falls only if cash discounts are lost, which is not automatic." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-8", question: "A business has a current (working capital) ratio of 4.00:1. What might this indicate?", choices: ["Too many resources are tied up in inventory, receivables or idle cash", "The business cannot pay its short-term debts", "The business is highly profitable", "The bank overdraft is very large"], explanation: "A very high current (working capital) ratio means current assets are far larger than needed to cover short-term debts, so money is sitting idle. It does not show profitability, and a large overdraft would make the ratio lower, not higher." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-9", question: "Closing inventory at the end of the year was overvalued by $3,000. What is the effect on the gross profit for the year?", choices: ["Gross profit is overstated by $3,000", "Gross profit is understated by $3,000", "Gross profit is unchanged but profit for the year is overstated", "Gross profit is overstated by $6,000"], explanation: "Closing inventory is deducted in cost of sales. If it is $3,000 too high, cost of sales is $3,000 too low and gross profit is $3,000 too high. Understatement is the effect of undervaluing inventory, the error does affect gross profit, and the error is counted once, not twice." },
    { id: "mcq-6.2-10", question: "A business made a profit for the year of $25,000, but its bank balance fell during the year. Which of the following could explain this?", choices: ["It bought a new delivery van for cash", "It charged depreciation on its equipment", "It received a long-term bank loan", "Its customers paid their accounts more quickly"], explanation: "Buying a van uses cash at once, while only depreciation is charged against profit, so cash can fall while a profit is made. Depreciation reduces profit but uses no cash, a loan brings cash in, and faster payment by customers increases cash." },
  ],
