  mcq: [
    { id: "mcq-6.4-1", question: "Which of the following is an internal user of accounting information?", choices: ["The manager of the sales department", "The business's bank", "A supplier who gives credit", "The tax authority"], explanation: "A manager works inside the business and uses detailed records to run it. The bank, a supplier and the tax authority are all outside the business and rely on the financial statements." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-2", question: "A supplier is deciding whether to sell goods on 30-day credit to a business. Which is the supplier most interested in?", choices: ["The business's ability to pay its short-term debts", "The business's return on capital employed", "The amount of the owner's drawings", "The gross profit margin earned by the business"], explanation: "The supplier wants to be paid on time, so liquidity is what matters. ROCE and gross profit margin measure profitability, and drawings concern the owner rather than the supplier." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-3", question: "A bank is considering a five-year loan to a business. Which figure would it examine first?", choices: ["Profit for the year before interest compared with the interest charge", "The rate of inventory turnover", "The trade receivables turnover in days", "The petty cash balance"], explanation: "A lender needs to know that the profit can cover the interest each year. Inventory and receivables ratios matter more to managers and suppliers, and petty cash is too small to influence a loan decision." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-4", question: "Why are employees interested in the financial statements of their employer?", choices: ["To judge job security and whether a pay rise can be afforded", "To calculate the tax the business must pay", "To decide whether to grant the business credit", "To compare the business's margins with their own business"], explanation: "Employees depend on the business continuing and being able to pay wages. Tax is the authority's concern, credit is the supplier's, and comparing margins is what a competitor does." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-5", question: "The tax authority uses a business's financial statements mainly to:", choices: ["Calculate the tax due on the profit for the year", "Decide whether to lend money to the business", "Judge whether the business will honour its guarantees", "Set the prices the business may charge"], explanation: "Tax is based on reported profit, so the authority uses profit for the year and checks the expenses. Lending is a bank's decision, guarantees concern customers, and the tax authority does not set prices." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-6", question: "A person thinking of buying shares in a company would be most interested in which ratio?", choices: ["Return on capital employed and its trend over several years", "Trade payables turnover in days", "The current (working capital) ratio only", "The rate of inventory turnover only"], explanation: "An investor wants to know what return the business earns on the funds invested and whether it is rising or falling. Trade payables turnover (days), the current (working capital) ratio and inventory turnover are useful but secondary for this decision." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-7", question: "A customer about to sign a long-term contract for supplies is most concerned that the business:", choices: ["Will continue to exist as a going concern to deliver the goods", "Pays its owner large drawings", "Has a high rate of inventory turnover", "Uses straight-line depreciation"], explanation: "The customer needs continuity of supply and after-sales service, so survival is the key question, answered by liquidity and profit trends. Drawings, turnover and depreciation methods do not directly affect the customer." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-8", question: "The owner of a sole trader business would use the financial statements to decide:", choices: ["Whether the return justifies the capital invested and how much can be taken as drawings", "How much tax other businesses pay", "Whether to give credit to the business", "Whether to lend money to the business"], explanation: "The owner has invested capital and labour and wants to know the return and what can safely be withdrawn. Giving credit and lending are decisions of suppliers and banks." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-9", question: "An environmental pressure group is studying the financial statements of a chemical company. What is it most likely to look for?", choices: ["Spending on pollution control and any fines for environmental damage", "The trade payables turnover (days), to decide whether to give credit", "The dividend per share, to decide whether to buy shares", "The rate of inventory turnover, to compare with its own business"], explanation: "An environmental body is concerned with the company's effect on the environment, so it looks for spending on pollution control, fines and whether the company can afford improvements. Credit decisions belong to suppliers, dividends to investors, and comparing inventory turnover is what a competitor does." },
    { id: "mcq-6.4-10", question: "The members of a tennis club want to judge whether the committee managed the club's money well during the year. Which statement should they examine first?", choices: ["The income and expenditure account, which shows the surplus or deficit for the year", "The receipts and payments account, which shows the surplus for the year", "The appropriation account, which shows how profit is shared", "The statement of changes in equity, which shows dividends paid"], explanation: "The income and expenditure account matches the year's income with the year's expenditure and shows the surplus or deficit, which is how members judge the committee. A receipts and payments account only summarises cash and shows a cash balance, not a surplus. Appropriation accounts and statements of changes in equity belong to partnerships and companies." },
  ],
